{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Requested the receipt that would make last turn's refutation receipt-backed, since it currently rests on draft-side operands. The harness now records, at every configuration-change append, the appending node's own log (index and EntryType, with transition and change list for ConfChangeV2 entries, capped at six entries), its pendingConfIndex and applied index before the proposal, whether its tracker was joint, and the DisableConfChangeValidation flag - so the decisive pair can be located by seed and step. The operands to confirm are the two appends at seed 35: the joint-remove change at index 2 proposed with applied=1 and pendingConfIndex=0, and the joint-add change at index 4 proposed with applied=3, pendingConfIndex=2 and joint=false while the appender's own log already contained the joint change at index 2. If the isolated run reproduces that pair, the rejected history is attributable to the harness's restart modelling - a node restored with a non-joint tracker over an applied joint change - and the refutation stands on execution rather than on my own workspace; if it does not, the operands will show which state actually produced the append and the availability question reopens. The request describes the instrumentation, states its assumptions (own-log summary read immediately after delivery, append events emitted for every configuration change, randomness fixed per seed, no production logic touched), and records that probes A-C and the three deterministic cases are unchanged along with the rejection operands and summary counters. No stop was declared: with the run's remaining time there is room for the receipt and a follow-up interpretation, and the open items remain the sweep's two gaps (the measurement check for configuration-change applicability and the earlier demotion/restart coverage) plus this receipt.\"}"}}
